  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A data set has mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=4$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.25.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=3.4.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{\u2015}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=4,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.25}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3.4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{\u2015}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3.4-4}{0.5} \\\\  & =-1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
